## Phoenix changes tracing setup to record spans from updated applications

Source: Arize Phoenix · GitHub — https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/releases/tag/%40arizeai/phoenix-otel%403.0.0
Published: 2026-11-11
Channel: github

Arize Phoenix is an AI observability platform for inspecting application traces and evaluation results. Its JavaScript tracing package moves to an updated OpenTelemetry tracing implementation so applications using that implementation can record spans after initialization. A span is the record of one operation within a trace.

First, applications call the package’s register function to obtain the tracing provider. The returned provider no longer has its own register method, so setup code must account for that changed interface. Next, custom span processors must come from the underlying tracing package and accept an options object. Phoenix also re-exports the batch and simple processor classes. A processor handles spans before they reach an exporter.

The migration changes configuration behavior as well: register no longer applies environment variables for sampling, span limits, attribute limits or batch processing. The release raises the minimum Node.js runtime and links a migration guide. Evaluation code that supplies its own tracing provider must use a compatible provider too. These are compatibility requirements, rather than a claim that upgrading automatically preserves an application’s previous telemetry settings.

Why it matters: For Phoenix JavaScript users: review the migration guide, verify processor and sampling configuration, and confirm a known operation appears in the stored trace.

