## AWS normalizes agent traces before comparing evaluation results

Source: Amazon Web Services — https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/uaef-a-framework-agnostic-system-for-closed-loop-evaluation-of-ai-agents/
Published: 2026-11-11
Channel: news

AWS’s Universal Agent Evaluation Framework is an open-source system for testing AI agents across supported frameworks. Frameworks record messages, tool calls and handoffs differently, which can tie evaluation logic to one implementation and complicate comparisons after a change.

First, an adapter converts the execution record into a common trace format. The same scoring engine then applies selected metrics to that record. It combines deterministic checks with model-based judges across dimensions including tool use, response quality and performance. Tests that need reference answers are skipped when those answers are absent. Tool-call checks need expected tools, ordering and arguments.

For comparisons over time, persisted experiments keep summary metadata in DynamoDB and full results in object storage. Reviewers can compare shared metrics against a baseline. Judge responses must cite text present in the scored content; unsupported evidence is rejected. AWS cautions that judge scores are evaluation signals and should not gate security controls. The library and deployable web interface share the engine.

Why it matters: For teams evaluating agents across frameworks: validate adapter coverage and references before comparing scores.

